      "notes": [
        {
          "on": "birchllc",
          "tone": "flag",
          "title": "Nothing on file moves Birch Avenue LLC into the trust.",
          "body": "It was formed in 2022, three years after the trust, and no paperwork moving it in is on file. Its agreement lists Sam as the only member (Sched. A) and names no one to step in if Sam dies (§ 9.2). Unless the trust’s own wording already reaches it, it goes through probate when Sam dies. The fix is a one-page assignment from the estate attorney, who will check the operating agreement and the loan first, since a transfer can need the lender’s okay."
        },
        {
          "on": "ira",
          "tone": "ask",
          "title": "Nothing on file says who gets Dana’s IRA.",
          "body": "The IRA statement has no beneficiary page. Ask the custodian for the current form, so what is on paper matches what would actually happen."
        }
      ],
      "gaps": [
        {
          "from": "birchllc",
          "zone": "in-trust",
          "label": "No assignment on file"
        }
      ],
      "kicker": "If you have a living trust and formed anything after it",
      "law": "Who owns what, as the deeds, operating agreements and account records show it. In Ohio, what is in one spouse’s name is that spouse’s."
    },
    {
      "id": "first-death",
      "type": "transfer",
      "fig": 1,
      "event": "sam-dies",
      "title": "When Sam dies, one rental would likely go through probate court.",
      "subtitle": "How each thing in Sam’s name would get to someone if Sam died first and Dana survived. Only the LLC needs a court, unless the trust’s own wording already reaches it.",
      "recipients": [
        "dana",
        "trust"
      ],
      "order": [
        "brokerage",
        "k401",
        "life",
        "home",
        "elmllc",
        "birchllc"
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "on": "birchllc",
          "tone": "flag",
          "title": "Only one thing on this page goes through a court.",
          "body": "Sam’s will sends Birch Avenue LLC to the trust, but only after probate: a public court process, often months long, with court and legal fees paid first. In Ohio, creditors alone get six months to file claims. Until the court names someone to act, no one may have clear authority to run the rental. Fig. 2 shows why it is outside the trust."
        },
        {
          "on": "trust",
          "tone": "info",
          "title": "“No court” doesn’t mean nothing to do.",
          "body": "Under the trust’s Art. 6, the whole trust carries on with Dana running it alone; many joint trusts instead split in two at the first death. A death still brings notices, a valuation of everything as of that date, and possibly an estate tax return that preserves Sam’s unused exemption. Which part counts as Sam’s depends on the trust’s terms and on who put what in, and that isn’t in the documents."
        },
        {
          "on": "dana",
          "tone": "ask",
          "title": "Any of Dana’s own forms that name Sam would need a new name.",
          "body": "After Sam’s death, Dana would update every account that names Sam. Her IRA has no beneficiary form in the documents at all, so that is the first one to check."
        }
      ],
      "bands": [
        "title",
        "contract",
        "trust",
        "probate"
      ],
      "kicker": "If you have a trust, a will and beneficiary forms",
      "fixes": {
        "birchllc": "a one-page assignment moving the LLC into the trust, after the attorney checks the operating agreement and the loan."
      },
      "law": "How things pass at a death: joint accounts to the other owner, accounts with a named person to that person, trust property under the trust’s own terms, and the rest through probate court (Ohio Revised Code, chapters 2113 to 2117)."
    }
